    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Two-week follow-up of cold-induced finger pain, seen the same day in neurology with nerve conduction studies. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Amundsen returns two weeks after his first visit, on the day of his neurology consultation with Dr. Abernathy-Dahl and nerve conduction studies this morning. The burning and numbness in his fingers come with every cold spell, on going outdoors, handling snow or cold metal, or washing in cold water, and last up to an hour after he comes indoors; the fingers turn white, then blue, then red as they rewarm. Between attacks the fingertips feel slightly numb. He has used liner gloves under mittens and hand warmers since the first visit, which helps. He has no weakness, no symptoms in his feet, and no wounds on the fingers. He does not want tablets that could make him drowsy. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Finger pain</td><td>Burning and numbness with cold, lasting up to an hour; color change</td></tr><tr><td>Between attacks</td><td>Slight fingertip numbness</td></tr><tr><td>Cold protection</td><td>Liner gloves and hand warmers help</td></tr><tr><td>Preference</td><td>No drowsy medicines</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

