    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-frostbite cold-induced neuropathic pain and cold sensitivity of the fingers (M79.2)</content>. The presenting finger symptoms now have their explanation. Nerve conduction studies this morning are normal, with no carpal or cubital tunnel entrapment and no large-fiber polyneuropathy, and the digital sensory responses are low-normal. The intake blood tests exclude the common other causes: HbA1c 5.4%, fasting glucose 94 mg/dL, vitamin B12 512 pg/mL, TSH 1.8 mIU/L, creatinine 0.96 mg/dL, ALT 24 U/L, hemoglobin 14.9 g/dL. With reduced pinprick and cold sensation at the fingertips, cold allodynia, and cold-induced color change, Dr. Abernathy-Dahl diagnoses a small-fiber neuropathy with vasospasm as a late sequela of his frostbite. Average pain 6 of 10 at intake. He wishes to avoid oral treatment, so capsaicin cream is the first choice under NICE CG173. [S]<list><item>Capsaicin 0.075% cream four times a day to the painful fingers.</item><item>Continue liner and heated gloves and hand warmers; warm the hands before fine work.</item><item>Review in four weeks; oral duloxetine if the cream is not enough.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain severity (0 to 10 numeric rating) and overall improvement</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Average pain 6 of 10 at intake two weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Evaluation for other causes of neuropathy</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>At intake: HbA1c 5.4%, fasting glucose 94 mg/dL, B12 512 pg/mL, TSH 1.8 mIU/L, creatinine 0.96 mg/dL, ALT 24 U/L, hemoglobin 14.9 g/dL; nerve conduction studies today normal</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Neurology consultation</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Abernathy-Dahl today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Skin of the fingers and cold protection</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No ulcers; liner gloves, mittens and hand warmers in use</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Daily activities and participation: fine hand tasks in the cold, driving, flying</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fumbles with buttons and keys in the cold; drives; no flying planned yet</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood (PHQ-9) and anxiety</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 5 at intake; mood fair today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Sleep quality</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Broken on cold nights by finger pain</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adverse effects, especially drowsiness, dizziness and nausea</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No medicine yet; capsaicin cream starts today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Average finger pain at or below 3 of 10, with fewer cold attacks, by four months. In progress; target 2026-06-09 [S]</item><item>Able to handle buttons, keys and a pen outdoors in the cold with protective gloves. In progress; target 2026-05-05 [S]</item><item>Any medicine used causes no daytime drowsiness that affects driving or flying. In progress; target 2026-06-09 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Neuropathic pain in adults: pharmacological management in non-specialist settings (CG173) (2013, updated 2020); Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Frostbite: 2024 Update (McIntosh SE, et al. Wilderness Environ Med 2024;35(2):183-197) (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in four weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-03-24: six-week review: finger pain on capsaicin cream, starting duloxetine. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
